data: {"type":"agent_chunk","agent":"researcher","content":"- CRISPR gene editing modifies the genomes of living organisms and is based on a simplified version of the bacterial CRISPR-Cas9 antiviral defense system. [1]\n- A Cas9 nuclease complexed with a synthetic guide RNA (gRNA) is delivered into a cell, which cuts the genome at a desired location. [1]\n- Genome editing inserts, deletes, modifies, or replaces DNA at site-specific locations, using molecular scissors that create double-strand breaks. [3]\n- In prokaryotes, CRISPR sequences derive from bacteriophage DNA and help detect and destroy similar phages in later infections. [2]\n- A 2021 headline linked CRISPR to treating a broad array of diseases [4], and a 2024 headline reported that HIV in cell culture was completely eliminated using CRISPR-Cas editing [5]. Both are headlines only, not verified findings."}

