data: {"type":"agent_chunk","agent":"analyst","content":"- **Mechanism:** A Cas9 nuclease guided by a synthetic gRNA cuts DNA at a chosen site [1]. Site-specific double-strand breaks then allow insertion, deletion, modification, or replacement of DNA [3].\n\n- **Biological origin:** The system is adapted from bacterial antiviral defense, where CRISPR sequences derive from phage DNA and help detect and destroy similar phages in later infections [1][2].\n\n- **Claims outpace evidence:** Headlines linking CRISPR to broad disease treatment (2021) and complete HIV elimination in cell culture (2024) are unverified and should not be taken as established results [4][5]."}

